  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Sweetgrass Tooth (Hydnellum suaveolens) is a small, delicate fungus in the Boletopsidaceae family characterized by its distinctive sweet, grass-like aroma. This woodland species produces a fruiting body with tooth-like spines beneath the cap instead of gills.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Sweet Grass Aroma",
      "description": "Emits a distinctive sweet fragrance reminiscent of fresh grass or hay, especially when crushed or dried.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spine Underside",
      "description": "Bears small, pale-colored spines running down the underside of the cap rather than traditional gills.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small Fruiting Body",
      "description": "Produces modest, delicate fruiting bodies that are typically brown to tan in color with a convex to flat cap.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in moist forests and woodlands throughout New England, typically growing on well-decomposed wood, leaf litter, or mossy areas in acidic soils.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The genus name Hydnellum refers to the tooth-like spines, and this species' sweet scent makes it one of the few fungi with a pleasant aroma notable enough to aid in field identification.",
